# libbox64dl patch 0021: guest-side application signal actions.
#
# The process-wide logical action (one current action per signal, shared by
# native and guest callers) and the kernel dispositions belong to
# src/signal_actions.c (patch 0020). This patch is the GUEST half declared in
# include/libbox64_signal_actions.h:
#
#   src/signal_guest.c   the active-delivery frame stack, the pending
#                        resume-mask handoff, the guest<->logical action
#                        conversion (libc 152-byte and kernel 32-byte
#                        rt_sigaction layouts, sigsetsize == 8), the stable
#                        guest-callable bridge for a NATIVE action, and the
#                        suspension wrapper that keeps the frames from dangling
#                        across a foreign longjmp.
#
# What changes in the engine, and why:
#
#   libtools/signals.c  every registration entry point (my_signal,
#                       __sysv_signal/sysv_signal, my_sigaction/__sigaction,
#                       my_syscall_rt_sigaction, my_sigset) is a view of the ONE
#                       logical action table. A guest query returns the ORIGINAL
#                       value -- the guest pointer for a GUEST action, a stable
#                       guest-callable bridge for a NATIVE one, SIG_DFL/SIG_IGN
#                       otherwise -- and a guest reinstall of that bridge lands
#                       back on the NATIVE action (never the bridge address
#                       stored as a guest handler, never a raw native pointer
#                       executed on the CPU). Invalid signals keep Linux's own
#                       errors: <=0 or >MAX_SIGNAL is EINVAL/SIG_ERR, and the C
#                       library's 32/33 are ENOTSUP (no path silently overwrites
#                       the C library's internal handler). SIGKILL and SIGSTOP
#                       cannot be caught, so a SET of either is EINVAL/SIG_ERR,
#                       while a QUERY of one is legal and reports the
#                       unchangeable default.
#
#                       my_sigactionhandler_oldcode(_64) takes the immutable
#                       action snapshot by value instead of reading
#                       my_context->signals/is_sigaction/restorer/onstack, and
#                       fills the synthetic guest uc_sigmask from the delivering
#                       native context instead of leaving it uninitialised.
#                       A guest uc_sigmask edit is honoured on BOTH exits: on the
#                       ordinary return it is applied to the bridge frame, and
#                       across a siglongjmp it is recorded for the resume hook
#                       so it wins over patch 0005's M0/M1 restore. The guest
#                       restorer is preserved for query round trips but NEVER
#                       executed natively.
#
#   os/emit_signals_linux.c  an emulator-generated signal (privileged
#                       instruction, INT n, divide by zero, repaired page) is
#                       delivered through the same logical table.
#
#   dynarec/dynarec.c   one hunk in the SigSetJmp resume branch: after patch
#                       0005 restores the frame's policy mask, apply the guest
#                       handler's recorded uc_sigmask edit and drop the
#                       abandoned guest delivery frames.
#
# NOT COMPILED-IN: the four fatal signals still reach the action module through
# patch 0020's dispatcher. This patch does not touch init/fini, the kernel entry
# variants or box64context.c.
